 2. Telling economic anecdotes
 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Tiffany Herring/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A grocery chain says it now costs more to ship a head of lettuce from California than to buy the lettuce itself. Tech companies are trying to purchase entire inventories of electrical equipment to build data centers.

 Why it matters: These anecdotes from the Fed's latest Beige Book show how two extraordinary forces — the AI buildout and the Iran war — are changing decisions about spending, investment and hiring.

 The implications extend beyond individual firms, with consequences for inflation and employment, as the Fed weighs another rate hike. Zoom in: A grocery chain told the Richmond Fed that diesel prices have risen 30% from a year earlier — making shipping lettuce more expensive than the cost of the vegetable — but price-sensitive customers have left it unable to pass along the higher costs.

 The energy shock is also squeezing businesses locked into earlier contracts: A Montana timber company said fuel costs had more than doubled on a project negotiated months earlier. The AI boom is creating another strain, intensifying competition for equipment needed to build data centers.

 An electrical equipment distributor told the Chicago Fed that tech companies tried to buy up all of its available equipment. Suppliers resisted, citing commitments to other customers. What to watch: AI is changing how many workers businesses believe they need and what they need them to do in ways not yet apparent in broader labor market data.

 In Dallas, an accounting industry contact said some large firms were delaying employment contracts because AI productivity gains could reduce their need for entry-level workers. A staffing firm in the area said AI agents conducted more than 180 hours of job interviews in two days, leaving human recruiters to review the results and make hiring decisions. 
 
